COURSE_DATA("is5",{"key":"is5","module":"IS","chapters":[{"id":"is-5","title":"Chapter 5 — IT Security / Cyber Security","title_en":"IT Security / Cyber Security","intro":["Chapter 5 is the most technical chapter of the ISA information security module. It has three sections: 5.1 Cryptography, 5.2 Operations Security, and 5.3 System acquisitions, requirement management and development. Its 15 controls cover encryption and transfer, change management, malware protection, logging, vulnerability patching, technical checks and network segmentation, then continuity, backup and recovery, and finally development, exit and tenant segregation for cloud and external IT services.","For automotive parts suppliers, almost every control in this chapter matches a step in a real ransomware playbook. The attacker comes in through an unpatched VPN (5.2.5), moves laterally through a flat, unsegmented network to the production line (5.2.7), switches off the antivirus (5.2.3) and deletes the domain-joined backups (5.2.9). And because there are no logs (5.2.4), the company cannot even explain what happened. After this chapter, you should be able to use this playbook the other way round to check the defenses of your own company.","ISA 2027 makes only a few real changes in this chapter: 5.2.3 deleted the should requirement on staff awareness (moved to 2.1.3), 5.2.8 turned the high protection needs items into separate requirements and adjusted the wording for very high protection needs, and the control question of 5.3.1 changed from IT system to IT service. But since ISA 6 the scope includes OT, so production networks, PLCs and HMIs are all in scope for this chapter."],"blocks":[{"type":"h3","text":"Overview of controls in this chapter"},{"type":"table","head":["Control","Topic","High / very high focus"],"rows":[["5.1.1","Management of cryptographic procedures","High: key sovereignty"],["5.1.2","Protection of information during transfer","High: at least transport encryption; very high: content encryption"],["5.2.1","Change management","High: verification during and after changes"],["5.2.2","Separation of development, test and operational environments","No additional high / very high requirements"],["5.2.3","Protection against malware","No additional high / very high requirements"],["5.2.4","Event logs","High: contractual requirements, remote access logging; very high: logging of all access"],["5.2.5","Identification and handling of vulnerabilities","No additional high / very high requirements"],["5.2.6","System and service audits","High: penetration tests of critical systems; very high: regular vulnerability scans"],["5.2.7","Network management and segmentation","High: device authentication, management interfaces, wireless and remote access"],["5.2.8","IT service continuity","High: RTO, SLAs, testing, backup protection; very high: standby systems"],["5.2.9","Backup and recovery","High: RPO / RTO, restore tests; very high: offline / immutable backups"],["5.3.1","Development and acquisition of IT services","Very high: penetration tests of custom software"],["5.3.2","Requirements for network services","High: network quality monitoring"],["5.3.3","Exit from external IT services","No additional high / very high requirements"],["5.3.4","Segregation in shared external IT services","No additional high / very high requirements"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Linking Chapter 5 with the ransomware playbook"},{"type":"olist","items":["Entry: the attacker exploits an unpatched VPN or firewall vulnerability, or steals an account by phishing → 5.2.5 vulnerability management, 5.2.6 technical checks, 5.1.2 MFA for remote access, 5.2.3 e-mail gateway protection.","Persistence and lateral movement: scanning in a flat network and obtaining domain admin rights → 5.2.7 network segmentation and detection of lateral movement, 5.2.4 logs and alerts.","Breaking the defenses: switching off antivirus and deleting backups → 5.2.3 tamper protection, 5.2.8 / 5.2.9 backup protection, offline and immutable backups, isolated IAM.","Encryption and extortion: servers, the MES and line workstations are encrypted → 5.2.8 continuity plan, RTO, coordinated communication with customers.","Recovery: restore from clean backups in the right order → 5.2.9 recovery sequence and restore tests; lessons learned afterwards → 5.2.1 change management and improvement."]},{"type":"callout","style":"warn","title":"OT production lines are the biggest blind spot of auto-parts plants","text":"In many plants, PLCs, CNC machines, measuring PCs and HMIs run old operating systems that cannot run antivirus or be updated, and they sit in the same network segment as the office. ISA accepts \"alternative measures\", but only if you have a list, a risk assessment and isolation. We recommend the Zones and Conduits concept of IEC 62443: start by putting a firewall between IT and OT."},{"type":"h3","text":"RPO and RTO at a glance"},{"type":"table","head":["Metric","Meaning","What it decides","Example"],"rows":[["RPO, Recovery Point Objective","The maximum amount of data loss (in time) you can accept","Backup frequency and replication method","ERP with an RPO of 1 hour: hourly transaction log backups"],["RTO, Recovery Time Objective","The latest time by which the service must be restored","Redundancy architecture, restore procedures and resources","MES with an RTO of 8 hours: needs a standby host and exercises"]]},{"type":"tip","who":"arale","text":"Let me write that down! The backup rule is 3-2-1-1-0: 3 copies of data, 2 types of media, 1 copy off site, 1 copy offline or immutable, and 0 restore errors. It matches the high and very high requirements of 5.2.9!"},{"type":"tip","who":"allan","text":"You do not need to buy expensive tools for every control in this chapter. Auditors look at four things: did you define requirements based on risk, did you implement them, do you have records, and do you keep reviewing them. A plant with three IT people can meet all must requirements with free tools plus good records."}],"controls":[{"id":"5.1.1","title":"Management of cryptographic procedures","q_en":"To what extent is the use of cryptographic procedures managed?","q_zh":"To what extent is the use of cryptographic procedures managed?","objective":"When using cryptographic methods, it is important to consider risks in the field of availability (lost key material) as well as those risks that arise due to incorrectly applied methods in the fields of integrity and confidentiality (poor algorithms/protocols or insufficient key strengths).","req":{"must":["All cryptographic procedures used (e.g., encryption, signature, and hash algorithms, protocols) provide the security required by the respective application field according to the recognized industry standard to the extent legally feasible."],"should":["A concept for the application of cryptography is defined and implemented. The following aspects are considered: – Cryptographic methods; – Key strengths; – Procedures for the complete lifecycle of cryptographic keys, including generation, storage, archiving, retrieval, distribution, deactivation, renewal, and deletion."],"high":["Key sovereignty requirements (particularly in case of external processing) are determined and fulfilled. (C, I)"],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.8.24 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 10.2.8 | BSI IT-Grundschutz CON.1, ORP.5 | NIST SP800-53r5 CM-3(6), SC-12, SC-13, SC-17, SC-28","change2027":"","explain":["The must requirement of this control is only one sentence, but it carries a lot of weight: **all** cryptographic procedures in use must provide the strength required by recognized industry standards. The scope is not only file encryption. It also covers digital signatures, hash algorithms and protocols, for example the TLS version of websites and VPNs, the WPA level of Wi-Fi, disk encryption algorithms and code-signing certificates. What auditors find most often is old equipment that still uses TLS 1.0 or 1.1, SMBv1, SHA-1 signatures, WEP or WPA-TKIP, or expired self-signed certificates. The phrase \"to the extent legally feasible\" is a reminder that some countries restrict the import, export or use of encryption. International groups need to pay attention to this.","The objective also mentions **availability** risks: a lost key means lost data. Many companies turn on BitLocker but do not store the recovery keys centrally. When the mainboard of a laptop fails, the data cannot be recovered. Or an SSL certificate expires without anyone noticing, and an external service goes down. This is why the should requirement asks for a **concept for the application of cryptography**. It must clearly state which algorithms are used, which key lengths, and the complete key lifecycle: generation, storage, archiving, retrieval, distribution, deactivation, renewal and deletion.","For high protection needs, **key sovereignty** is required. When data is processed externally (for example in a cloud service or an outsourced data center), you must first determine who controls the keys, and then make sure this requirement is met. A common approach is customer-managed keys (BYOK or CMK) or an HSM for cloud services, so that the provider cannot decrypt the data without the customer's authorization. Suppliers who handle design data with high protection needs on behalf of an OEM are often asked about this.","Links to other controls: 5.1.2 covers encryption in transit, while this control covers the management of the cryptographic mechanisms themselves. Authentication under 4.1.x uses certificates and keys. If backups under 5.2.9 are encrypted, you must make sure the keys are available during a restore. Note: ISO 27001:2022 A.8.24 points in the same direction, so you can use one common \"policy on the use of cryptography\" for both."],"practice":["Write an **Encryption and Key Management Policy and Procedure**: list the approved algorithms and minimum strengths (for example AES-256, RSA 3072 or higher or ECC P-256 or higher, SHA-256 or higher, TLS 1.2 or higher with TLS 1.3 preferred), and define a list of forbidden ones (DES, 3DES, RC4, MD5, SHA-1, SSLv3, TLS 1.0 and 1.1).","Create a **cryptographic asset inventory** with these fields: system name, purpose (transfer, storage or signature), algorithm and key length, certificate issuer, expiry date, key custodian, storage location (AD, Key Vault, HSM, safe). Review it at least once a year.","Full-disk encryption on endpoints: on Windows, enforce BitLocker with Intune or GPO and back up recovery keys automatically to Entra ID or AD. On macOS, enable FileVault through MDM and escrow the recovery key. For USB sticks, use BitLocker To Go or hardware-encrypted drives.","Certificate lifecycle management: use a spreadsheet or a certificate monitoring tool (for example a PKI management platform, or certificate expiry alerts in your monitoring system) to send a renewal notice 30 days in advance. Specify that private keys must never be sent by e-mail.","Regularly check the encryption protocols and cipher suites of external websites, VPNs and mail servers with vulnerability scanners or SSL test tools (for example Nessus, OpenVAS, testssl.sh). Feed the results into vulnerability handling under 5.2.5.","For cloud services (M365, Azure, AWS, Google Workspace), assess whether customer-managed keys (CMK or BYOK) or double key encryption are needed. For projects with high protection needs, define key ownership and access conditions in the contract.","Key handover and deletion: when people leave or systems are decommissioned, follow the procedure to revoke certificates, rotate shared keys and destroy private keys that are no longer used. Keep records of this."],"audit":{"questions":["Which cryptographic mechanisms does your company use? Is there a list?","How do you decide that these algorithms and key lengths meet industry standards? What is your reference?","Where are the recovery keys for laptop disk encryption stored? Who can access them? Are there access records?","Which TLS versions do your external websites and VPN currently support? Have old protocols been disabled?","How do you keep track of certificate expiry? When was the last certificate renewed?","What is the defined process for keys from generation to deletion? What happens to the keys a person holds when they leave the company?","For data with high protection needs in the cloud, who controls the keys? Can the provider decrypt the data on its own?"],"evidence":["Encryption and key management policy (including lists of approved and forbidden algorithms)","Cryptographic asset and certificate inventory with expiry tracking records","Intune or GPO BitLocker compliance reports, screenshots of recovery key escrow","TLS and cipher scan reports with improvement records","Screenshots of cloud key management settings (Key Vault, KMS, CMK) or contract clauses","Records of key revocation, rotation and destruction"],"findings":["External websites and the VPN still allow TLS 1.0 or 1.1 or weak ciphers, and this is not part of any improvement plan.","BitLocker is enabled on laptops, but users keep the recovery keys themselves. After a mainboard failure the data could not be recovered.","The company has no encryption policy. Algorithms are simply the default of each system, and no one can explain whether they meet the standard.","An expired SSL certificate caused an outage of the supplier portal, which shows there is no certificate lifecycle management.","Data with high protection needs is stored in the cloud, but key sovereignty was not assessed and is not covered in the contract."]},"tip":"Encryption only counts when the keys are well managed. I have seen too many plants with BitLocker turned on everywhere, and the recovery keys sitting in an Excel file of an employee who already left. First take inventory, then escrow the keys centrally, and only then discuss algorithms."},{"id":"5.1.2","title":"Protection of information during transfer","q_en":"To what extent is information protected during transfer?","q_zh":"To what extent is information protected during transfer?","objective":"When being transferred via public or private networks, information can in some circumstances be read or manipulated by unauthorized third parties. Therefore, requirements regarding the protection needs of the information must be determined and implemented by taking suitable measures during such transfer.","req":{"must":["The network services used to transfer information are identified and documented.","Policies and procedures in accordance with the classification requirements for the use of network services are defined and implemented.","Measures for the protection of transferred contents against unauthorized access are implemented."],"should":["Measures for ensuring correct addressing and correct transfer of information are implemented.","Electronic data exchange is conducted using content or transport encryption according to the respective classification.","Remote access connections to the organization's network possess adequate security features. The following aspects are considered: – Encryption; – Authentication strength; – Granting and termination of access and capabilities; – Using a secured connection (e.g., VPN)."],"high":["Information is transported or transferred in encrypted form (at least transport-encryption) or protected by similarly effective measures. (C)"],"vhigh":["Information is transported or transferred in content-encrypted form. (C)"],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.5.14 | NIST CSF 2.0 PR.DS-02, PR.IR-01 | BSI IT-Grundschutz NET.1.1, CON.1, CON.9, OPS.1.1.7 | NIST SP800-53r5 SC-8, SC-23, SC-37","change2027":"","explain":["The first must requirement is to **identify and document** the network services used to transfer information. Typical transfer channels in the automotive supply chain are e-mail, OEM supplier portals, ENX or OFTP2 EDI, SFTP, cloud sharing (OneDrive, Google Drive), instant messaging (Teams, WhatsApp, LINE), VPN, and even USB sticks sent by courier. Many companies only discover that sales, R&D and quality each use their own channels when they are asked, \"Which channels do you use to exchange drawings with customers?\" You need the list first. Only then can you define, by classification, what may be sent through each channel.","The key point of this control is the difference between **transport encryption** and **content encryption**. Transport encryption protects the channel, for example TLS, VPN or SFTP. Once the data arrives on the other server, it is plain text. Content encryption protects the file itself, for example S/MIME, PGP, an encrypted ZIP (AES-256) or Microsoft Purview encryption labels. Even if the mail server or the cloud is compromised, no one can read the content without the key. High protection needs require at least transport encryption or a similarly effective measure. Very high protection needs require content encryption.","The should requirements also ask for **correct addressing and correct transfer**, in other words preventing e-mails to the wrong person and sending the wrong file. In practice you can reduce the risk of misdirected e-mails with warnings for external recipients, management of auto-complete lists, DLP rules, and links with access control instead of large attachments. For remote access (VPN, remote desktop, remote maintenance by vendors), you must consider encryption, authentication strength (for example MFA), and granting and termination of access. This connects with access control under 4.1.x.","A common misunderstanding is \"We have a VPN, so everything is secure.\" A VPN only protects the tunnel between the company and the remote side. If the data is then sent to a customer through a personal Gmail account or a chat app, the requirement is still not met. Another misunderstanding is writing the password of an encrypted ZIP in the same e-mail. That is the same as no encryption. The password must be sent through a different channel (for example SMS or a phone call)."],"practice":["Create an **inventory of information transfer channels** with these fields: channel name, purpose, counterpart (customer, supplier, internal), highest classification allowed, encryption type (transport or content), owner, approval status.","Add a transfer rules table to your **Information Classification and Handling Rules**. For example: \"Internal\" may be sent by company e-mail. \"Confidential (high)\" requires enforced TLS or an approved company cloud sharing link limited to named recipients. \"Strictly confidential (very high)\" requires content encryption (S/MIME, Purview encryption labels, or an AES-256 encrypted archive with the password sent through another channel).","Configure the mail server to enforce TLS (for example Forced TLS connectors in Exchange Online for the domains of key customers), and enable SPF, DKIM and DMARC to prevent spoofing.","Deploy DLP (Microsoft Purview DLP, Google Workspace DLP) to detect outgoing e-mails that contain customer part numbers, project code names or personal data, and to warn or block. Enable a warning banner for external recipients.","All remote access must go through VPN or ZTNA with MFA enforced. Accounts are opened only with an approved request form and are disabled when a person leaves or a project ends. Remote maintenance by vendors is \"opened only when needed, time-limited, and fully recorded\".","Prohibit sending classified company information through personal mailboxes, personal cloud storage and unapproved messaging apps, and explain the reasons in security awareness training."],"audit":{"questions":["Which channels do you use to exchange data with customers and suppliers? Are they documented?","What are the transfer requirements for each classification level? Please show me an e-mail that sends a confidential drawing.","Is TLS enforced for e-mail transfer? How do you verify this?","How do you apply content encryption to data with very high protection needs? How are keys or passwords transmitted?","How do you prevent e-mails being sent to the wrong recipients?","Which technology do you use for remote access? Is there MFA? How are accounts requested and disabled?","Does anyone use chat apps or personal cloud storage to send company files? How do you control this?"],"evidence":["Inventory of information transfer channels with approval records","Section on transfer requirements in the information classification and handling rules","Screenshots of mail server TLS, SPF, DKIM and DMARC settings","DLP policy settings and alert records","VPN and MFA settings, records of remote access account requests and deactivations","Usage records or examples of content encryption tools (S/MIME, Purview labels)"],"findings":["Information transfer channels were never inventoried, and R&D engineers exchange drawings with customers through personal cloud storage.","The password of an encrypted archive is written in the same e-mail as the file.","Data from a project with high protection needs is sent by e-mail without enforced TLS, and no similarly effective measure was assessed.","MFA is not enabled for the VPN, and VPN accounts of former employees still work.","Data with very high protection needs is protected only by transport encryption, without content encryption."]},"tip":"Ask sales, R&D and quality to open their own Sent Items folder and look at three days of e-mail. Then you will know the real transfer channels of your company. The list is always longer than you think, and that is exactly the point of the first must requirement."},{"id":"5.2.1","title":"Change management","q_en":"To what extent are changes managed?","q_zh":"To what extent are changes managed?","objective":"The objective is to ensure that information security aspects are considered in case of any changes to the organization, business processes and IT systems (Change Management) in order to prevent these changes from causing an uncontrolled reduction in the information security level.","req":{"must":["Information security requirements for changes to the organization, business processes, IT systems are determined and fulfilled."],"should":["A formal approval procedure is established.","The potential impact on the information security of changes is assessed.","Changes affecting the information security are subjected to planning and testing.","Procedures for fallback in fault cases are considered."],"high":["Compliance with the information security requirements is verified during and after the changes are applied. (C, I, A)"],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.8.32 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 7.2.4 | NIST CSF 2.0 PR.PS-01, PR.PS-04 | BSI IT-Grundschutz ORP.5, OPS.1.1.2, OPS.1.1.3 | NIST SP800-53r5 CM-2, CM-3, CM-9, SA-10","change2027":"","explain":["Pay attention to the scope: it is not only changes to IT systems, but also changes to the **organization** and to **business processes**. For example, merging departments, adding a new production line, outsourcing the warehouse, introducing a new MES or moving the server room can all affect the security level. The must requirement asks you to determine and fulfill the security requirements for these changes in advance. This prevents situations like \"after the change we found all firewall rules wide open\" or \"the new plant area has no access control\".","The should requirements describe a complete change process: formal approval, assessment of the security impact, planning and testing, and a fallback plan. **Fallback** is especially important. For example, if a firewall firmware upgrade fails, you must be able to go back to the old version. If an ERP upgrade fails, you need a restore point. High protection needs add one more requirement: you must verify **during and after** the change that the security requirements are still met, for example by rescanning for vulnerabilities and checking that permissions and logging still work correctly.","In small and medium-sized plants, the most common situation is an IT team of only two or three people, where changes are made after a quick word or a message in a chat group. ISA does not require you to buy an ITSM system, but you need traceable records: who requested the change, why, what the risk is, who approved it, when it was done, what the result was, and how to roll back if something goes wrong. Emergency changes may be done first and documented later, but this must be defined in the procedure, and approval must be obtained afterwards.","Links to other controls: 5.2.2 (separation of environments, where testing takes place), 5.2.5 (a patch is itself a change), 5.3.1 (new development or acquisition of IT services) and 1.3.4 (software approval). Changes in the OT environment (for example PLC program changes or HMI updates) should also be included, with downtime windows agreed with production, plant engineering and quality."],"practice":["Write a **Change Management Procedure** with three categories: normal changes, standard changes (pre-approved routine work, such as the monthly Windows patches) and emergency changes. Define the approval level for each.","Fields in the change request form: change number, requester, description and reason, affected systems, security impact assessment (C, I, A), test plan, fallback plan, planned time and downtime window, approver, result, and verification result after the change.","Use a tool to keep records: Jira Service Management, ServiceNow, Redmine, GLPI, or at least a shared SharePoint list. Do not discuss changes only in a chat group.","For major changes, hold a CAB (Change Advisory Board) or have the security contact co-sign. For organizational and process changes (such as outsourcing, relocation or a new plant), the ISMS manager performs a risk assessment.","After changes to systems with high protection needs, run a verification checklist: vulnerability scan, permission comparison, check that logs still reach the SIEM, check that backups succeed, and compare firewall rule differences.","OT changes (PLC, HMI, SCADA) must be coordinated with production. Test first on a spare unit or in a simulation environment, keep a backup of the program before the change, and record the change in the equipment history."],"audit":{"questions":["Please describe your change management process, from request to closure.","Please show me a recent change record for a firewall or server.","How do you assess the security impact before a change? Who is responsible for this?","Where is the fallback plan if a change fails? Have you ever actually used it?","How are emergency changes handled? How is approval obtained afterwards?","Do changes to the organization or processes (for example outsourcing or relocation) also go through this process?","After a change is completed, how do you confirm that the security requirements are still met?"],"evidence":["Change management procedure","Change request forms or tickets (including approval, testing, fallback and verification)","CAB meeting minutes or co-signature records","Post-change verification records (vulnerability scans, configuration comparisons)","Risk assessment records for organizational or process changes"],"findings":["Firewall rule changes have no request or approval records. The network administrator changes them on his own.","Change forms are approved, but there are no fields for security impact assessment or fallback plan.","When a new production line was moved to the plant, no security risk assessment was done, and the new equipment was connected directly to the office network.","The share of emergency changes is too high, and approval was never obtained afterwards.","After a change to a system with high protection needs, the security settings were not verified. As a result, log collection stopped for several weeks without anyone noticing."]},"tip":"In audits, my favorite question is: what did you change last week? If the IT manager has to scroll through chat messages to answer, change management is not yet on track. The records do not need to be fancy, but you must be able to find them."},{"id":"5.2.2","title":"Separation of development, testing and operational environments","q_en":"To what extent are development and testing environments separated from operational environments?","q_zh":"To what extent are development and testing environments separated from operational environments?","objective":"The objective of separating the development, testing and operational environments is to ensure that the availability, confidentiality and integrity of productive data are maintained.","req":{"must":["The IT systems have been subjected to risk assessment in order to determine the necessity of their separation into development, testing and operational systems.","A segmentation is implemented based on the results of risk analysis."],"should":["The requirements for development and testing environments are determined and fulfilled. The following aspects are considered: – Separation of development, testing and operational systems; – No development and system tools on operational systems (except those required for operation); – Use of different user profiles for development, testing, and operational systems."],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.8.31 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 6.3.3 | BSI IT-Grundschutz CON.8, CON.10 | NIST SP800-53r5 CM-4(1), SA-17(6)","change2027":"","explain":["The must requirement of this control is **risk-based**. Not every system needs three environments. You first perform a risk assessment to decide which systems need separation, and then implement segmentation based on the result. For example, a self-developed MES, an external supplier portal or customized ERP programs usually need a test environment, but a simple file server may not. What matters is that there is an assessment, a conclusion and implementation.","The should requirement names three concrete aspects: separation of environments, no development or system tools on operational systems, and different accounts for different environments. \"No development tools\" means that production servers should not have Visual Studio, compilers, database administration tools or network tools that attackers like to use. This reduces what an attacker can exploit. \"Different accounts\" prevents a developer from accidentally running a command in production with the same account.","In a plant, the same idea applies to OT: PLC program changes should first be verified offline or in a simulator, not changed directly online. A new MES version should be verified on a test station before it is deployed to the line. This control is closely connected with change management under 5.2.1 and secure development under 5.3.1. If production data is used for testing, see the requirements for test data in 5.3.1."],"practice":["Create an **environment separation risk assessment sheet**: list each system, whether it is self-developed or customized, how often it changes, and the protection needs of its data. The conclusion is \"three environments needed\", \"two environments needed (test and production)\" or \"no separation needed\", with the reason.","For systems that need separation, build the environments on different VMs, different database instances or different cloud subscriptions or projects, and separate the development and test network segments from production with firewalls or VLANs.","Keep production accounts and development accounts separate: by default, developers have no write access to production, and deployment is done by operations staff or a CI/CD pipeline. Use different AD groups or naming rules (for example adm- and dev-).","Define a baseline configuration for production servers, remove unnecessary development tools, compilers and admin tools, and compare regularly with configuration scans or the software inventory.","OT environment: test PLC and HMI program changes first on spare units or in simulation software, separate the engineering workstation from the production network segment, and have production confirm before going live.","Use version control (Git) and a deployment pipeline, so that the path \"development, test, production\" has traceable records."],"audit":{"questions":["Which systems have separate development, test and production environments? On what basis did you decide?","Please show me the results of the risk assessment for environment separation.","Can developers access the production database directly? With which account?","Are development tools installed on production servers? How do you check this?","What is the process for moving a program from test to production? Who performs the deployment?","Where are program changes for OT systems (PLC, HMI) tested?"],"evidence":["Environment separation risk assessment sheet","Network diagram (showing development, test and production segments)","Account and permission lists (development vs. production)","Software inventory of production servers or baseline configuration comparison records","Deployment records or CI/CD pipeline logs"],"findings":["The company claims that no test environment is needed without any risk assessment, but the MES is often changed directly on the production machine.","Developers use the same administrator account to log in to both the test and the production database.","SQL administration tools and network scanning tools are installed on production servers, with no explanation of an operational need.","The test and production environments are in the same network segment, and a test program wrote data into production by mistake."]},"tip":"Writing \"no separation needed\" in the risk assessment is a valid answer, but you must be able to explain why. The worst case is when nothing is written down and engineers change programs directly on the production line computers."},{"id":"5.2.3","title":"Protection against malware","q_en":"To what extent are IT systems protected against malware?","q_zh":"To what extent are IT systems protected against malware?","objective":"The objective is to both technically and organizationally ensure the protection of IT systems against malware.","req":{"must":["Requirements for protection against malware are determined.","Technical and organizational measures for protection against malware are defined and implemented."],"should":["Unnecessary network services are disabled.","Access to network services is restricted to necessary access through suitable protective measures (see examples).","Malware protection software is installed and updated automatically at regular intervals (e.g., virus scanner).","Received files and software are automatically inspected for malware prior to their execution (on-access scan).","The entire data contents of all systems are regularly inspected for malware.","Data transferred by central gateways (e.g., e-mail, internet, third-party networks) is automatically inspected by means of protection software. The following aspects are considered: – Encrypted connections; – Encrypted content.","Measures to prevent protection software from being deactivated or altered by users are defined and implemented.","For IT systems operated without the use of malware protection software, alternative measures (e.g., special resilience measures, few services, no active users, network isolation) are implemented."],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.8.7 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 9.2.1, 9.3.1, 9.3.2, 9.3.3 | BSI IT-Grundschutz OPS.1.1.4, DER.1, OPS.1.1.2 | NIST SP800-53r5 CM-7(2), RA-10, SC-35, SI-3","change2027":"ISA 2027 deleted the should requirement \"Case-related staff awareness measures\", because this requirement is already covered by control 2.1.3 (information security awareness).","explain":["The must requirements are short: first determine the protection requirements, then define and implement technical and organizational measures. The should requirements are a complete list. They cover **reducing the attack surface** (disabling unnecessary services, restricting access), **endpoint protection** (automatic antivirus updates, on-access scanning, regular full scans), **gateway protection** (automatic inspection of e-mail, internet and third-party networks), **tamper protection** (users cannot switch off antivirus), and alternative measures for **systems that cannot run antivirus**.","Gateway protection specifically mentions **encrypted connections** and **encrypted content**. Today almost all web traffic is HTTPS. If the firewall or proxy does not inspect TLS, malware can pass straight through. Encrypted archives are also often used to avoid e-mail scanning. In practice, you can decide based on risk whether to use SSL inspection (taking privacy and legal requirements into account). For encrypted attachments that cannot be scanned, use a quarantine or blocking policy.","The last should requirement is very important for plants. OT equipment (for example HMIs on old Windows XP or 7, measuring instrument PCs, CNC controllers) often cannot run antivirus, or the manufacturer does not allow it. ISA accepts alternative measures, such as network isolation, application whitelisting, disabled USB ports, only necessary services, no normal user logins, or a USB scanning station. What matters is that you have a list, an assessment and alternative measures.","A common misunderstanding is \"Having antivirus installed is enough.\" Modern ransomware first tries to switch off antivirus, so tamper protection and behavior detection by EDR are becoming more and more important. Also, the fact that the 2027 version deleted the awareness requirement here does not mean you no longer need to do it. It is now checked in one place, under 2.1.3."],"practice":["Write a **Malware Protection Rule**: define the scope of installation of protection software, update frequency, scan schedules, the alert handling process and exception approval.","Deploy EDR or antivirus on endpoints (for example Microsoft Defender for Endpoint, Trend Micro Apex One, Sophos, CrowdStrike). Use the central console to confirm that signatures and engines update automatically, on-access scanning is enabled and a weekly full-disk scan is scheduled.","Enable tamper protection. Normal users have no local administrator rights, so they cannot disable or uninstall the protection software.","On the mail gateway (Exchange Online Protection or Defender for Office 365, Google Workspace, a secure e-mail gateway), enable attachment sandboxing, URL rewriting and a policy for encrypted archives. On the internet firewall, enable IPS, antivirus and URL filtering, and decide the scope of TLS inspection based on a risk assessment.","Use a baseline configuration on servers and endpoints to disable unnecessary services (for example SMBv1, Telnet, unused RDP), and open only the necessary ports in the host firewall.","Create a **list of systems that cannot run antivirus**: record the device, the reason and the alternative measures (isolated VLAN, whitelisting, USB blocking, read-only accounts, regular offline scans), approved by the person responsible for information security.","Set up a USB scanning station (kiosk). USB sticks brought in by external vendors must be scanned before they are connected to production equipment.","Review the protection console reports every month: devices not updated, devices offline, detections and how they were handled."],"audit":{"questions":["Where are your malware protection requirements written down?","Please open the antivirus or EDR console and show me the signature update status and the number of offline devices.","Can a normal user switch off the antivirus? Please try it on this computer.","How are e-mail attachments and internet downloads inspected? How do you handle encrypted archives?","Are there any devices that cannot run antivirus? What are the alternative measures?","How do you handle USB sticks from external vendors?","When was malware last detected? How was it handled?"],"evidence":["Malware protection rule","EDR or antivirus console compliance reports (update rate, scan schedules)","Screenshots of tamper protection settings and the list of local administrator rights","E-mail and web gateway protection settings","List of devices that cannot run antivirus, with approval records for alternative measures","Malware incident handling records"],"findings":["The antivirus console shows several computers whose signatures have not been updated for more than a month, and no one follows up.","Users have local administrator rights and can disable the antivirus software themselves.","Production HMIs run an old Windows version without antivirus, and no alternative measures such as network isolation are in place.","The mail gateway lets through encrypted archives that cannot be scanned, without any compensating measures.","Servers are not scanned regularly with full-disk scans."]},"tip":"When I audit a plant, I always ask: does that old computer on the production line have antivirus? If the answer is no, that is fine, but you must be able to show me the list of alternative measures. The worst case is when everyone pretends that computer does not exist."},{"id":"5.2.4","title":"Recording and analysis of event logs","q_en":"To what extent are event logs recorded and analysed?","q_zh":"To what extent are event logs recorded and analysed?","objective":"Event logs support the traceability of events in case of a security incident. This requires that events necessary to determine the causes are recorded and stored. In addition, the logging and analysis of activities in accordance with applicable legislation (e.g., Data Protection or Works Constitution Act) is required to determine which user account has made changes to IT systems.","req":{"must":["Information security requirements regarding the handling of event logs are determined and fulfilled.","Security-relevant requirements regarding the logging of activities of system administrators and users are determined and fulfilled.","The IT systems used are assessed regarding the necessity of logging.","When using external IT services, information on the monitoring options is obtained and considered in the assessment.","Event logs are checked regularly for policy violations and noticeable problems in compliance with the permissible legal and organizational provisions."],"should":["A procedure for the escalation of relevant events to the responsible body (e.g., security incident report, data protection, corporate security, IT security) is defined and established.","Event logs (contents and meta data) are protected against alteration (e.g., by a dedicated environment).","Adequate monitoring and recording of any actions on the network that are relevant to information security are established."],"high":["Information security requirements relevant to the security during the handling of event logs, e.g., contractual requirements, are determined and implemented. (C, I, A)","Events related to establishing and terminating remote access sessions (e.g., for remote maintenance) are logged. (C, I, A)"],"vhigh":["Logging of any access to data of very high protection needs is performed as far as technically feasible and as permissible according to legal and organizational provisions. (C, I)"],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.5.17, A.8.15 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 10.2.5, 12.2.4, 12.2.5 | NIST CSF 2.0 PR.PS-04, DE.AE-02, DE.AE-03, DE.AE-04, DE.AE-08, DE.CM-01, DE.CM-09, DE.AE-06 | BSI IT-Grundschutz OPS.1.1.5, DER.2.2, DER.2.3 | NIST SP800-53r5 AC-2(4), AC-2(7), AC-2(12), AC-6(9), PE-8, AU-2–AU-8, AU-11, AU-12, SC-43, SI-4, SI-11","change2027":"","explain":["This control has five must requirements, which can be summarized as three tasks: **assess, record, check**. First assess which systems need logging and what must be logged (especially the activities of system administrators). When you use cloud or external services, find out first which logs and monitoring functions they offer (for example, the retention period of M365 audit logs depends on the license). Finally, and this is the most often forgotten part, you must **check the logs regularly** for policy violations and anomalies. Collecting logs without looking at them is the most common audit finding.","The objective mentions the German Works Constitution Act, because Germany has strict rules on employee monitoring. In other countries, check your national data-protection law (for example the EU GDPR, or Taiwan's PDPA) and labor regulations. When logs contain personal data of employees, internal rules should explain the purpose and scope of monitoring, restrict access, and limit the retention period. The text repeats \"as permissible according to legal and organizational provisions\" several times. The aim is a balance between security traceability and privacy.","The should requirements ask for an escalation procedure (linked with incident management under 1.6.x), protection of logs against alteration (for example by sending them to a separate log server or SIEM where administrators cannot delete them), and monitoring of security-relevant actions on the network. High protection needs require implementing contractual requirements for logs (for example a retention period required by the OEM) and logging the start and end of remote access sessions (including remote maintenance by vendors). Very high protection needs require logging of **any access** to data with very high protection needs.","A SIEM is not mandatory. A small company can start with centralized logging, for example Windows Event Forwarding, Wazuh or Graylog, together with a weekly review checklist. When there is budget, you can introduce Microsoft Sentinel, Splunk or an outsourced SOC or MDR service. The key is that you can answer this question: when something happens, can I find out who did what, and when?"],"practice":["Write a **Log Management Procedure**: define the list of systems to be logged, event types (successful and failed logins, permission changes, administrator actions, firewall blocks, VPN connections, backup results, antivirus alerts), retention period (for example at least 6 to 12 months, adjusted to contracts and laws), review frequency and the responsible person.","Create a **system logging requirements assessment sheet**: for each system, mark whether logging is needed, what is logged, where logs are stored, whether they go to the SIEM, and the log functions and limits of external services.","Collect logs centrally: send logs from AD, firewalls, VPN, EDR, M365 or Google Workspace audit logs, servers and important OT gateways to a SIEM (Wazuh, Graylog, Sentinel, Splunk) or an outsourced SOC.","Protect log integrity: put the log server in its own network segment with separate accounts, give administrators no delete rights, enable WORM or immutable storage, and synchronize time with NTP.","Set up alert rules: repeated failed logins, administrator logins outside working hours, new domain administrators, mass deletion or encryption of files, unusual outbound connections. Alerts are reported to the security contact according to the escalation procedure.","Review logs weekly or monthly and keep records (reviewer, date, findings, actions). Increase the frequency for systems with high protection needs.","For remote maintenance by vendors, use a jump server or PAM (for example CyberArk, Teleport, a remote access gateway). Record the start and end time of each connection, and record the session on video if needed."],"audit":{"questions":["Which systems did you assess as needing logging? Please show me the assessment results.","Are administrator actions logged? Can administrators delete their own records?","How long are logs kept? On what basis?","Who looks at the logs? How often? Please show me the record of the last review.","Which logs do your cloud services provide? Have you enabled them?","Where are the connection records for remote maintenance by vendors?","When you find an unusual event, how do you escalate it?"],"evidence":["Log management procedure and system logging requirements assessment sheet","Screens of the SIEM or log server, list of alert rules","Records of regular log reviews and handling of anomalies","Log protection settings (permissions, immutable storage, time synchronization)","Settings showing that cloud service audit logs are enabled","Remote access connection records"],"findings":["Logs are collected, but there are no records of regular reviews. A brute-force attack on an account went unnoticed for several weeks.","Domain administrators can delete records on the log server. The logs are not protected against alteration.","The retention period of M365 audit logs was never assessed, and the data was no longer available during an incident investigation.","There are no connection records for remote maintenance by vendors, so no one knows who connected to production systems and when.","Server clocks are not synchronized, so logs from different systems cannot be compared."]},"tip":"Logs are like a dashcam in your car. No one cares about it until something happens, and then if you do not have it, you are in trouble. But remember, someone also needs to look at the recordings regularly. Otherwise it is just an expensive hard drive."},{"id":"5.2.5","title":"Identification and handling of vulnerabilities","q_en":"To what extent are vulnerabilities identified and addressed?","q_zh":"To what extent are vulnerabilities identified and addressed?","objective":"Vulnerabilities increase the risk of IT systems being unable to meet the requirements for confidentiality, availability and integrity. Exploitation of vulnerabilities is among the possible ways for attackers to gain access to the IT system or to threaten its operating stability.","req":{"must":["Information on technical vulnerabilities for the IT systems in use is gathered (e.g., information from the manufacturer, system audits, CVE database) and evaluated (e.g., Common Vulnerability Scoring System CVSS).","Potentially affected IT systems and software are identified and the risk caused by the vulnerability is assessed.","Risks arising from vulnerabilities are treated."],"should":["An adequate patch management is defined and implemented (e.g., patch testing and installation).","Risk minimizing measures are implemented, as necessary.","Successful installation of patches is verified in an appropriate manner."],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.8.8, A.8.19 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 9.4.2, 12.2.9 | NIST CSF 2.0 ID.RA-01, ID.RA-08 | BSI IT-Grundschutz OPS.1.1.2, OPS.1.1.3, IND.1 | NIST SP800-53r5 RA-5, CM-8, RA-10, SA-11, SC-31, SI-2, SI-4, SI-5","change2027":"","explain":["The must requirements describe a complete vulnerability management cycle: gather information, evaluate severity, identify affected assets, assess the risk and treat the risk. Sources can be manufacturer advisories (for example Microsoft Patch Tuesday, Fortinet PSIRT, Siemens ProductCERT), CVE and NVD, alerts from your national CERT (for example CERT-EU, BSI CERT-Bund, or TWCERT/CC in Taiwan), the CISA KEV catalog (Known Exploited Vulnerabilities) and vulnerability scan results. To identify affected systems, you need a complete hardware and software inventory (linked with asset management under 1.3.1).","CVSS is the scoring method given as an example in the text. Scores go from 0 to 10, and the usual levels are Low, Medium, High (7.0 and above) and Critical (9.0 and above). But CVSS only shows technical severity. Risk also depends on how important the asset is, whether it is exposed to the internet and whether a public exploit exists. In practice, we recommend defining patch deadlines in the policy, for example handling Critical vulnerabilities on internet-facing systems first, and allowing adjustments based on risk.","**Treating the risk** does not always mean installing a patch. OT equipment may not be updatable because of manufacturer certification. In this case you can reduce the risk with network isolation, disabling services, virtual patching (IPS rules) and stronger monitoring, and management accepts the residual risk. The should requirements ask that patch management includes testing and installation, risk-reducing measures where needed, and verification that patches were really installed (for example WSUS or Intune compliance reports, or a rescan).","The original audit questions include: what information about technical vulnerabilities can be determined, what your sources are, where hardware and software are listed, how you reconcile this with your IT systems, when you respond to identified vulnerabilities, and how the process from identification to elimination is defined. When you prepare, make sure you have a document or record to answer each of these questions."],"practice":["Write a **Vulnerability and Patch Management Procedure**: define information sources, the evaluation method (CVSS plus asset importance and exposure), deadlines, the exception and risk acceptance process, and how you verify.","Create a list of vulnerability information sources and assign an owner: manufacturer advisories, NVD and CVE, CISA KEV, your national CERT, alerts from OEMs or the industry ISAC. Summarize them once a week.","Example patch deadlines (set your own based on risk): Critical vulnerabilities on internet-facing systems or listed in KEV as soon as possible (for example within 7 days), High within 30 days, Medium within 90 days, Low during routine maintenance.","Distribute patches centrally with WSUS, Intune, SCCM, Ansible or an RMM tool. Verify them first in a pilot group, then deploy in waves. Include third-party software (browsers, PDF readers, Java).","Produce a monthly patch compliance report and vulnerability scan results, identify unpatched devices and follow up until closure.","OT equipment: check with the manufacturer which patches and certified versions are available. For devices that cannot be patched, use compensating measures such as an isolated VLAN, firewall allow lists, virtual patching, disabled USB ports and disabled unnecessary services, and record them in the risk register.","Track the firmware of network devices, VPNs and firewalls separately. Internet-facing devices like these are the entry point that ransomware groups use most often."],"audit":{"questions":["From which sources do you get information on technical vulnerabilities?","Where is your hardware and software inventory? How do you match it with vulnerability information?","How do you evaluate the severity of vulnerabilities? Do you use CVSS or another method?","What is the process and the deadline from discovering a vulnerability to completing the patch?","Please show me last month's patch compliance report. How do you handle unpatched devices?","How do you reduce the risk of devices that cannot be patched (for example production line computers)? Who approves the risk acceptance?","For the most recent critical vulnerability (for example in a VPN or firewall), when did you learn about it and when was it fixed?"],"evidence":["Vulnerability and patch management procedure","Records of vulnerability information gathering and evaluation","Hardware and software asset inventory","Patch compliance reports (WSUS, Intune, RMM)","Vulnerability scan reports and tracking list","Approval records for exceptions and risk acceptance"],"findings":["There is no fixed source of vulnerability information. The company relies only on what vendor sales people tell them.","A VPN device has had a publicly exploited vulnerability for more than two months without a patch or any compensating measure.","Windows updates automatically, but third-party software and network device firmware are not part of patch management.","Patches are not verified after installation, and reports show that installation has failed on several devices for a long time.","Production line computers cannot be updated, but the risk is not registered and no isolation is in place."]},"tip":"Ransomware rarely uses some mysterious zero-day attack. Most of the time it comes in through a VPN or firewall that someone forgot to update. Make the firmware of internet-facing devices your top priority. That gives you the best value for the effort."},{"id":"5.2.6","title":"System and service audits (technical checks)","q_en":"To what extent are IT systems and services technically checked (system and service audit)?","q_zh":"To what extent are IT systems and services technically checked (system and service audit)?","objective":"The objective of technical checks is to detect any issues which can jeopardize the confidentiality, integrity or availability of IT systems and services.","req":{"must":["Requirements for auditing IT systems or services are determined.","The scope of the system audit is specified in a timely manner.","System or service audits are coordinated with the operator and users of the IT systems or services.","The results of system or service audits are stored in a traceable manner and reported to the relevant management.","Measures are derived from the results and are implemented in an appropriate timeframe."],"should":["System and service audits are planned taking into account any security risks they might cause (e.g., disturbances).","Regular system or service audits are performed. The following aspects are considered: – Audits are carried out by qualified personnel; – If applicable, suitable tools (e.g., vulnerability scanners) are used; – Audits are performed from the internet and the internal network.","Within a reasonable period following completion of the audit, a report is prepared."],"high":["For critical IT systems or services, additional system or service audit requirements have been identified and are fulfilled (e.g., service specific tests and tools and/or human penetration tests, risk-based time intervals). (A)"],"vhigh":["IT systems and services are regularly scanned for vulnerabilities. Suitable protective measures must be implemented for systems and services that may not be scanned. (C, I, A)"],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.5.36, A.8.34, A.8.8 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 6.3.2 | NIST CSF 2.0 ID.IM-02, DE.CM-09, ID.RA-09 | NIST SP800-53r5 RA-5, AU-5, AU-6, CA-7, CM-4, PL-10, PL-11, RA-6, SI-6, SI-7, SI-15, SR-10, SR-11","change2027":"","explain":["The \"audit\" in this control is a technical check, such as a vulnerability scan, configuration check or penetration test. It is not the internal ISMS audit (that is 1.5.x). The must requirements ask you to determine audit requirements, define the scope in time, coordinate with the operators and users of the systems, store results traceably and report them to management, and finally derive measures from the results and implement them in an appropriate timeframe. In other words, a scan report that goes into a drawer is not acceptable.","**Coordination** and **considering security risks** are especially important in plants. A vulnerability scan can crash an old PLC, HMI or network printer and stop the production line. This is why the should requirements ask you to consider the risk of disturbance when planning, for example by scanning during maintenance downtime, using passive monitoring for OT, or test-scanning a small range first. The should requirements also ask for regular audits by qualified personnel with suitable tools, from both the **internet** and the **internal network**.","High protection needs require more intensive audits of critical systems, for example manual penetration tests and service-specific test tools (web application scanning, AD security checks), with intervals based on risk. Very high protection needs require regular vulnerability scans, and for systems that cannot be scanned, suitable protective measures **must** be implemented. The examples in the original text are a desktop firewall and linking services to loopback interfaces only, so that the service is not open to the network.","This control works together with 5.2.5: 5.2.6 **finds** problems, and 5.2.5 **handles** them. Findings in the report should go into the vulnerability tracking list and connect with the patch process."],"practice":["Write a **Technical Security Testing Plan**: each year, schedule the scope, frequency, tester and timeline for external vulnerability scans, internal vulnerability scans, configuration checks and penetration tests.","Example scanning tools: Nessus, Qualys, OpenVAS or Greenbone, Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management, PingCastle (AD security check). For web systems you can use OWASP ZAP or Burp Suite.","Recommended frequency (adjust based on risk): external systems scanned monthly or quarterly, internal systems quarterly, and for critical systems a third-party penetration test at least once a year or after major changes.","Before scanning, send a notice to system owners and users with the time, scope, possible impact and an emergency contact. For OT segments, confirm with production first, and use passive monitoring tools (for example an OT-specific IDS) or scan during downtime.","Submit the report within two weeks after completion (or your own defined deadline). It includes a summary, risk ratings, affected systems, recommendations and owners, and is presented in the management review or the security committee.","Enter findings in the tracking list, link them with the patch deadlines from 5.2.5, and verify in the next scan whether they are closed.","Systems that cannot be scanned: restrict sources with the host firewall, bind services to localhost only, isolate the network segment and record them in the exception list."],"audit":{"questions":["Which technical checks have you performed on your IT systems? How often?","How do you decide the scan scope? Does it cover both external and internal?","How do you inform system owners before a scan? How do you avoid affecting production?","What qualifications do the testers or the vendor have?","Please show me the latest report and the record showing that management received it.","What is the status of the high-risk items in the report?","Have critical systems had a penetration test? How do you protect systems that cannot be scanned?"],"evidence":["Technical security testing plan or annual schedule","Vulnerability scan and penetration test reports","Scan notices and coordination records","Minutes of meetings where results were reported to management","Findings tracking list and closure records","Tester certificates or qualification evidence of the external vendor"],"findings":["Only external scans are performed. The internal network has never been checked.","Scan reports were not reported to management, and high-risk items have not been handled for six months.","A scan without prior coordination crashed an HMI on the production line.","Critical systems have never had a penetration test, and no additional audit requirements were defined.","OT devices that cannot be scanned are not tracked and have no protective measures."]},"tip":"The worst kind of scan report is \"one hundred pages, five hundred findings, zero closed\". Start with the top ten internet-facing and high-risk items, make the numbers go down in the next scan, and management will understand it."},{"id":"5.2.7","title":"Management of the organization's network","q_en":"To what extent is the network of the organization managed?","q_zh":"To what extent is the network of the organization managed?","objective":"IT systems in a network are exposed to different risks or have different protection needs. In order to detect or prevent unintended data exchange or access between these IT systems, they are subdivided into suitable segments and access is controlled and monitored by means of security technologies.","req":{"must":["Requirements for the management and control of networks are determined and fulfilled.","Requirements regarding network segmentation are determined and fulfilled."],"should":["Procedures for the management and control of networks are defined.","For a risk-based network segmentation the following aspects are considered: – Limitations for connecting IT systems to the network; – Use of security technologies; – Performance, trust, availability, security, and safety considerations; – Limitation of impact in case of compromised IT systems; – Detection of potential attacks and lateral movement of attackers; – Separation of networks with different operational purpose (e.g., test and development networks, office network, manufacturing networks); – The increased risk due to network services accessible via the internet; – Technology-specific separation options when using external IT services; – Adequate separation between own networks and customer networks while considering customer requirements; – Detection and prevention of data loss/leakage."],"high":["Extended requirements for the management and control of networks are determined and implemented. (C, I, A) The following aspects are considered: – Authentication of IT systems on the network; – Access to the management interfaces of IT systems is restricted; – Specific risks (e.g., wireless and remote access)."],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.8.20, A.8.22 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 6.3.2, 8.2.1, 8.2.2, 8.2.6, 8.2.8 | NIST CSF 2.0 ID.AM-03, PR.IR-01, PR.IR-03 | BSI IT-Grundschutz NET.1.1, NET.1.2 | NIST SP800-53r5 SC-5, SC-6, SC-7, SC-10, SC-11, SC-20–SC-22, SC-24–SC-27, SC-29, SC-30, SC-32, SC-36, SC-38–SC-40, SC-44, SC-48, SI-23","change2027":"","explain":["This is one of the core controls of the IT security chapter. The must requirements are only two sentences: determine and fulfill the requirements for network management and control, and determine and fulfill the requirements for network segmentation. The should requirements list ten aspects to consider for segmentation, which you can use as a **segmentation design checklist**. Possible measures in the original examples include firewalls, intrusion detection and prevention systems (IDS/IPS), network management tools and network security software that prevents unintended data exchange.","For a Tier-2 auto-parts plant, the biggest topic is **separating IT and OT**. In many plants, production PLCs, CNC machines, coordinate measuring machines, MES workstations and office computers are all in one flat network. If one sales laptop is infected with ransomware, it can spread laterally to the production line and stop production. We recommend the Zones and Conduits concept of IEC 62443 and the Purdue model. At a minimum, separate the office network, server zone, OT production network, guest Wi-Fi, management segment and DMZ, and put a firewall and an IT/OT DMZ between IT and OT (for example for the MES data exchange server, the jump server and the patch relay server).","Some should aspects deserve special attention. **Detecting lateral movement** means that after segmentation you must still be able to see unusual traffic between zones. **Separation between own networks and customer networks** is common when you work on site at a customer plant or connect to a dedicated OEM network (for example the ENX network or a customer VPN). **Detection and prevention of data leakage** can be combined with DLP. **Safety** is a reminder that OT firewall rules must not affect safety interlocks and similar mechanisms.","The extended requirements for high protection needs include authentication of devices on the network (for example 802.1X or NAC, so that unauthorized devices cannot use the network even when plugged in), restricted access to management interfaces (management interfaces of switches, firewalls and servers can only be reached from the management segment or a jump server), and handling of specific risks such as wireless networks and remote access. The original audit questions also ask how you identify new devices in the network that were not installed by IT, what rights administrators have on firewalls, switches and routers, how the network is documented, and what the requirements are when using cloud services."],"practice":["Draw and maintain a **network diagram** and a **network segment list**: show VLANs, IP ranges, purpose, protection needs, security zones and firewall locations. Update them after every change (linked with 5.2.1).","Example segment design: office segment, server segment, management segment, OT production segment (which can be split further by production line), IT/OT DMZ, external DMZ, guest Wi-Fi, IoT (cameras, access control), and a dedicated segment for R&D or prototypes.","Firewall rules follow \"deny by default, allow by allow list\". Between IT and OT, only necessary connections are allowed (for example specific ports to the MES server). Review the rules every six months and remove outdated rules.","Introduce NAC or 802.1X, or at least MAC address binding and shutdown of unused switch ports. Compare regular network scans with the asset inventory to find devices that were connected without permission.","Management interfaces can only be reached from the management segment or a jump server. Disable Telnet and HTTP and use SSH and HTTPS. Integrate network device admin accounts with RADIUS or TACACS+ and enable MFA.","Deploy IDS or IPS, or passive OT-specific monitoring (for example Nozomi, Claroty, Microsoft Defender for IoT, TXOne), to detect lateral movement and unusual protocols.","Wireless networks: employee Wi-Fi uses WPA2 or WPA3 Enterprise, and guest Wi-Fi is fully isolated with internet access only. Wi-Fi for AGVs and handheld scanners in the plant uses its own SSID and VLAN.","Write a **Network Management Procedure** that covers adding devices, configuration backups, firmware updates, approval of rule changes, sending logs to the SIEM and regular reviews."],"audit":{"questions":["What requirements have you defined for managing and controlling your network? How do you check that they are followed?","Please show me the network diagram and explain how the segments are separated. What was the basis for this assessment?","How are the office network and the production network isolated from each other? How do you prevent access across segments?","How do you find devices that were connected to the network without permission and not installed by IT?","Who manages the firewalls, switches and routers? What rights do the administrators have? How do they access the management interfaces?","Which network protocols do you use, and which have you disabled? Which protection mechanisms do you use, for example firewalls or IDS and IPS?","What are the availability requirements? How is the network documented? What are the requirements when using cloud services or externally hosted systems?","How do you handle visitors, vendors and wireless networks?"],"evidence":["Network diagram and network segment list (with date and version)","Network segmentation risk assessment or design description","Firewall rule set and records of regular reviews","NAC or 802.1X settings, or records of detecting unauthorized devices","List of network device admin accounts and access method settings","IDS, IPS or OT monitoring alert records","Network management procedure"],"findings":["The office network and the production network are not separated, so ransomware could spread directly to the MES and PLCs.","The network diagram is three years old and does not match the current situation.","The firewall has any-any allow rules, and there are no review records.","Guest Wi-Fi can reach the internal server segment.","The switch management interface can be reached by Telnet from any office computer and uses the default password.","Unauthorized devices cannot be detected. On the day of the audit, an unregistered wireless access point was found in a meeting room."]},"tip":"Network segmentation is like fire compartments in a plant: in a fire, they stop the flames from spreading from the warehouse to the production line. You do not need to make it perfect at once. Just build the wall between IT and OT first, and you have already blocked more than half of the ransomware playbook."},{"id":"5.2.8","title":"Continuity planning for IT services","q_en":"To what extent is continuity planning for IT services in place?","q_zh":"To what extent is continuity planning for IT services in place?","objective":"Data and IT services can become unavailable through events such as hardware failures, software defects, operator errors or attacks. Backup and recovery enables organizations to recover from relevant situations and limit potential harm to the organization to a reasonable amount.","req":{"must":["Critical IT services are identified, and business impact is considered.","Requirements and responsibilities for continuity and recovery of those IT services are known to relevant stakeholders and fulfilled."],"should":["Critical IT systems are identified. The following aspects are considered: – Classification of relevant systems to have the appropriate protection need; – Implementation of adequate and appropriate security measures.","Continuity planning exists and is regularly reviewed and updated. The following aspects are considered: – Alternative communication strategies, in case primary communication means are not available; – Alternative storage strategies, in case primary storage means are not available; – Alternative power supply and network connection.","Continuity planning includes at least (distributed) denial of service attacks, successful ransomware attacks and other sabotage activities, system failure scenarios, and natural disaster scenarios affecting critical IT systems."],"high":["Continuity planning includes predefined time frames (Recovery Time Objective) for resumption of operation in line with requirements. (A)","Appropriate SLAs with external service providers according to continuity planning are in place. (A)","Continuity plans include coordination of contractually agreed communication with business partners. (A)","Continuity planning is regularly tested including a full recovery and reconstitution of the system to a known state and compliance with defined target times. (A)","A backup and recovery strategy for critical IT services and information is defined and implemented. (C, I, A)","Backups of critical IT services and information are sufficiently protected against unauthorized modification or deletion by malicious software. (I, A)","Backups of critical IT services and information are sufficiently protected against unauthorized access by malicious software or operators. (C, I)"],"vhigh":["Continuity planning is coordinated with the continuity plans of relevant external service providers. (A)","Continuance of essential mission and business functions with minimal or no loss of operational continuity is possible. The following aspects are considered: – Alternate operation strategies and necessary separated standby systems to retain and/or resume operation to the extent possible if critical IT services become unavailable (A); – Alternate and backup storage sites (if they exist) must provide controls equivalent to that of the primary site (C, I, A).","Continuity planning is tested regularly. Test scenarios, results, and lessons learned are recorded. (I, A)"],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.5.30 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 13.2.1 | NIST CSF 2.0 PR.IR-02 | BSI IT-Grundschutz DER.4, INF.1 | NIST SP800-53r5 CP-2, CP-9, CP-9(3), PE-8, PE-13(2)","change2027":"This control was added in ISA 6.0. ISA 2027 changed each requirement for high protection needs from a sub-point (–) into a separate requirement (+), so each one must be fulfilled on its own. For very high protection needs, the former wording \"tests and lessons learned are documented\" was changed to \"results and lessons learned are recorded\" (the current text: test scenarios, results and lessons learned are recorded).","explain":["5.2.8 covers **business continuity at the level of IT services**, one level above the backups of 5.2.9. The must requirements ask you to identify critical IT services and consider the business impact (the idea of a BIA), and to make sure relevant people know the requirements and responsibilities for continuity and recovery. For an auto-parts plant, critical IT services usually include the ERP (orders, shipping), the MES (production traceability), EDI or customer portals (receiving call-offs), e-mail, AD authentication, and in OT the SCADA and line control systems.","The should requirements name specific scenarios: DDoS, successful ransomware and other sabotage, system failure and natural disasters. Include the local hazards of your site, such as earthquakes, typhoons or storms, power outages and floods. Alternative strategies cover communication (if e-mail is down, how do you contact customers? For example a backup mobile chat group or a backup cloud mailbox), storage (if the NAS fails, where does the data go?), power (UPS, generator) and network (two ISPs, a 4G or 5G backup line).","ISA 2027 turned the seven requirements for high protection needs into separate \"+\" items, so you must have evidence for each one: a clear RTO, SLAs with external service providers, coordination of communication with business partners such as customers (for example an OEM contract that requires notification of a supply interruption within a certain time), regular full recovery tests, a backup and recovery strategy, and protection of backups against modification or deletion by malware and against unauthorized access by malware or operators. The last two target the ransomware tactic of attacking the backups first.","Very high protection needs require coordination with the continuity plans of external service providers, separated standby systems and backup sites with equivalent controls, and records of test scenarios, results and lessons learned. Note: this can be integrated with crisis management under 1.6.3 and with ISO 22301 business continuity management, but the focus of this ISA control is IT services."],"practice":["Perform a **business impact analysis (BIA) for IT services**: list the business processes each IT service supports and the impact of one hour, one day and three days of downtime (financial, delivery dates, customer penalties, safety). Use this to set the RTO, RPO and priorities.","Write an **IT Continuity Plan**: include roles and the contact list, activation criteria, response steps for each scenario (ransomware, DDoS, server room fire, earthquake, long power outage, ISP outage), alternative communication and alternative ways of working (for example paper route cards when the MES is down).","Ransomware playbook: isolation steps, decision makers, external notifications (customers, ENX or OEM as required by contract, authorities), and the order of restoring from clean backups (AD, then core network, then ERP and MES, then the rest).","Sign SLAs with external service providers (data center, cloud, ISP, MES vendor) that state availability, response and repair times and notification duties, and ask for a summary of their BCP so you can coordinate.","Run an exercise at least once a year: a tabletop exercise plus a real restore test. Record the actual recovery time, compare it with the RTO and define improvements.","Power and network redundancy: regular UPS discharge tests, a monthly generator test run, two ISPs or a mobile network backup, and environmental monitoring of the server room."],"audit":{"questions":["Which are your critical IT services? How did you decide?","If the ERP or MES stops, how is the business affected? How long can you tolerate it?","Does your continuity plan cover ransomware, DDoS, system failure and natural disasters?","If neither e-mail nor phone works, how do you contact customers and employees?","What is the RTO? How long did recovery actually take in the last exercise?","Do the SLAs of your external service providers match your RTO?","How do you prevent backups from being encrypted or deleted by ransomware? Who can access the backups?"],"evidence":["IT service BIA and list of critical services","IT continuity plan and ransomware response playbook","Exercise plan, exercise records and improvement tracking","SLAs with external service providers and BCP coordination records","Communication coordination with customers (contact points and deadlines for notification)","Backup protection settings (immutable storage, isolated accounts)"],"findings":["There is a business continuity plan, but it does not cover a ransomware scenario.","The RTO is set at 4 hours, but it has never been tested. In reality, restoring the ERP takes two days.","The repair times in the SLAs of the cloud and data center providers are much longer than the company's RTO, and the gap was never assessed.","The backup server is joined to the domain. After a domain administrator account was stolen, the backups were deleted as well.","The continuity plan is three years old, and many of the contact persons have already left the company."]},"tip":"In your exercise, please bring a stopwatch. I once worked with a company that wrote an RTO of four hours. In the real exercise, they found that just finding the key to the backup tape cabinet took two hours. A number only counts after you have tested it."},{"id":"5.2.9","title":"Backup and recovery of data and IT services","q_en":"To what extent is the backup and recovery of data and IT services ensured?","q_zh":"To what extent is the backup and recovery of data and IT services ensured?","objective":"Data and IT services can become unavailable through events such as hardware failures, software defects, operator errors or attacks. Backup and recovery enables organizations to recover from relevant situations and limit potential harm to the organization to a reasonable amount.","req":{"must":["Backup concepts exist for relevant IT systems. Appropriate protective measures to ensure confidentiality, integrity, and availability for data backups are considered.","Recovery concepts exist for relevant IT services."],"should":["A backup and recovery concept exists for each relevant IT service. Dependencies between IT services and the sequence for recovery are considered."],"high":["Backup and recovery concepts are methodically reviewed at regular intervals. (A)","General restore capability is considered and tested (e.g., sample testing, test systems). (I, A)","Backup and recovery concepts exist. (A) The following aspects are considered: – Recovery Point Objective (RPO); – Recovery Time Objective (RTO); – Required resources for recovery (considering capacity and performance incl. personnel and hardware); – Avoidance of overload scenarios during recovery; – Appropriate spatial redundancy (e.g., separate room, separate fire section, separate data center, separate site)."],"vhigh":["(Additional) Backups are performed via offline procedures, immutable backups or by using an isolated IAM solution. (I, A)","Restore procedures are technically tested in a methodical way at regular intervals. (I, A)","Geographical redundancy is considered in data backup and recovery concepts. (A)"],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.8.13 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 13.3.1, 13.3.2, 13.3.3, 13.3.4, 13.3.5 | NIST CSF 2.0 PR.DS-11, RC.RP-03 | BSI IT-Grundschutz CON.3, OPS.1.2.2 | NIST SP800-53r5 CP-2, CP-9, CP-9(3), PE-8, PE-13(2)","change2027":"","explain":["The must requirements have two key points. The **backup concept** applies to IT systems and must consider the C, I and A protection of the backups themselves (a backup is a complete copy of company data and needs encryption and access control). The **recovery concept** applies to IT services. Many companies have a backup schedule but have never thought about recovery: to which machine do we restore? Who does it? What do we restore first? The should requirement goes further and asks you to consider dependencies between services and the recovery sequence. For example, the ERP depends on AD and the database. If AD is not back, nothing else can be restored.","High protection needs require the RPO (how much data loss you can accept, which decides the backup frequency), the RTO (how quickly the service must be back), the resources needed for recovery (people, hardware, bandwidth), avoidance of overload during recovery (for example a hundred VMs restoring from the same NAS at the same time), and spatial redundancy (backups must not be in the same rack or the same fire section as the production systems). The concepts must also be reviewed regularly, and the general restore capability must be tested.","Very high protection needs respond directly to ransomware. Additional backups must be offline (for example tapes or rotating external disks), immutable (for example Object Lock, WORM, a hardened Linux repository), or use an isolated IAM (backup system accounts are not part of the company AD, so even an attacker with domain admin rights cannot delete them). In addition, restore procedures must be technically tested at regular intervals, and geographical redundancy must be considered (for example another plant site or another cloud region).","In practice, people often quote the **3-2-1-1-0 rule**: at least 3 copies of the data, on 2 different media, 1 copy off site, 1 copy offline or immutable, and 0 errors (restores verified without errors). This is not in the ISA text, but it matches the high and very high requirements of this control very well. For SaaS services (for example M365), assess whether you need a third-party backup, because the provider's retention features are not the same as a backup."],"practice":["Create a **Backup and Recovery Procedure** and a **backup inventory** with these fields: system or service, data content, protection needs, backup type (full, incremental, snapshot), frequency, retention period, RPO, RTO, storage location (local, off site, cloud, offline), encryption, owner, dependent services and recovery sequence.","Use a 3-2-1-1-0 architecture: a local backup appliance (for fast restores), plus an off-site or cloud copy, plus an immutable or offline copy (for example a Veeam Hardened Repository, S3 Object Lock, or tapes rotated to a safe deposit box).","Give the backup system its own accounts and network segment, do not join it to the domain, enable MFA on the management interface, encrypt backup files and keep the encryption keys centrally (linked with 5.1.1).","Do a sample restore test every month (a single file, a single database), and at least once a year a full service restore exercise (for example restoring AD and the ERP in an isolated environment). Record the actual time taken and compare it with the RTO.","OT backups: include PLC programs, HMI project files, machine parameters, SCADA configurations and engineering workstation images in the backup inventory. Store them on a backup server in the OT segment and keep an additional offline copy.","SaaS backups: assess the retention policies of M365 or Google Workspace and introduce a third-party SaaS backup if needed.","Check backup job results every day and open a ticket for every failure. Review once a year whether the backup concept still meets business needs."],"audit":{"questions":["Please show me the backup inventory. How did you decide the backup frequency and retention period of each system?","What are the RPO and RTO? How do you verify that you can meet them?","Where are the backups stored? Are they in the same server room or fire section as the production systems?","If a domain administrator account is hacked, can the attacker delete the backups?","When was the last restore test? What did you restore? How long did it take?","When several services need to be recovered, how is the order planned?","Are PLC programs and machine parameters on the production line backed up?"],"evidence":["Backup and recovery procedure, backup inventory","Backup job logs and records of failure handling","Restore test records (including time taken and results)","Screenshots of immutable or offline backup settings, records of off-site tape storage","Backup system account and access permission settings","Records of regular reviews of the backup concept"],"findings":["There is only a backup schedule. A restore has never been tested.","The backup NAS is in the same rack as the file server and is accessed with a domain account. In a ransomware incident, it was encrypted as well.","Service dependencies and the recovery sequence were not defined. In an exercise, the ERP could not start because AD had not been restored.","PLC programs exist only on the personal laptop of an engineer.","Data with very high protection needs has no offline or immutable backup."]},"tip":"A backup that has never been tested is only a hope. Restore one file every month, and restore one complete service every year. That is my minimum requirement for every client."},{"id":"5.3.1","title":"Information security in new or further developed IT services","q_en":"To what extent is information security considered in new or further developed IT service?","q_zh":"To what extent is information security considered in new or further developed IT service?","objective":"Information security is an integral part of the entire lifecycle of IT service. This particularly includes consideration of information security requirements in the development or acquisition of IT service.","req":{"must":["The information security requirements associated with the design and development of IT service are determined and considered.","The information security requirements associated with the acquisition or extension of IT service and IT components are determined and considered.","Information security requirements associated with changes to developed IT services are considered.","System approval tests are carried out under consideration of the information security requirements."],"should":["Requirement specifications are prepared. The following aspects are considered: – The information security requirements; – Vendor recommendations and best practices for secure configuration and implementation; – Best practices and security guidelines; – Fail safe (designed to return to a safe condition in the event of a failure or malfunction).","Requirement specifications are reviewed against the information security requirements.","The IT service is reviewed for compliance with specifications prior to productive use.","The use of production data for testing purposes is avoided as far as possible (if applicable, anonymization or pseudonymization). The following aspects are considered: – Requirements for the lifecycle of test data (e.g., deletion, maximum lifetime on the IT service); – Case-related specifications for the generation of test data are defined.","Test systems are provided with protective measures comparable to those in the operational environment where productive data are used for testing purposes."],"high":[],"vhigh":["The security of purpose built software or significantly customized software is tested (e.g., penetration testing) during commissioning, in case of significant changes, or at regular intervals. (C, I, A)"],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.5.8, A.8.25 | NIST CSF 2.0 PR.PS-01, PR.PS-06 | BSI IT-Grundschutz CON.8, CON.10, OPS.1.1.2, OPS.1.1.6 | NIST SP800-53r5 SA-3, SA-4, SA-5, SA-8, SA-20, SI-13, SR-5","change2027":"In the control question, \"IT system\" was changed to \"IT service\". This stresses that the scope covers the whole service (including cloud services, outsourced development and procurement), not just a single system. Note: in ISA 5, this control was created by merging the former controls 14.2 and 14.3.","explain":["The idea of this control is **Security by Design**. Whether you develop in house, outsource development, buy packaged software or subscribe to a cloud service, you must write the security requirements into the requirements from the start and verify them before going live. The 2027 version changed \"IT system\" to \"IT service\" as a reminder that the scope is wider. For example, introducing a new SaaS quotation system, extending an MES module or buying software for new inspection equipment are all in scope.","The must requirements cover four points in time: design and development, acquisition or extension, changes, and approval testing. The should requirements explain how: prepare requirement specifications (including security requirements, the vendor's secure configuration recommendations, best practices and fail-safe design), review the specifications, and confirm compliance with the specifications before going live. **Fail safe** is very real in a plant. For example, if the interface between the MES and a PLC is lost, the equipment should stop in a safe state instead of running on uncontrolled.","Test data is a common finding. Engineers copy the production ERP database directly to a test machine. It contains customer prices and personal data of employees, but the test machine has no protection at all. The should requirements ask you to avoid production data as far as possible, to anonymize or pseudonymize it if needed, and to define deletion and retention periods for test data. If you really do use production data, the test system must have protection comparable to the production environment.","Very high protection needs require security testing of purpose-built or significantly customized software, for example penetration testing or source code analysis. The original audit questions also ask whether you use a development standard or framework (for example OWASP ASVS or a Secure SDLC), how you control outsourced development, how you manage the development environment (commissioning, configuration, access, decommissioning), and how you assess and document development risks. This control connects with 5.2.1, 5.2.2 and supplier management under 6.1.x."],"practice":["Write a **Procedure for Security Requirements in System Development and Procurement**: every new system, new SaaS or module extension needs a completed **security requirements checklist** before procurement or development can start.","Example fields in the security requirements checklist: data classification and protection needs, authentication (SSO, MFA), permission design, logging, encryption, backup, responsibility for vulnerability patching, data storage location, exit and return of data (linked with 5.3.3), vendor secure configuration recommendations, fail-safe design.","Add security clauses to procurement contracts and RFPs: secure development requirements, vulnerability notification and patch deadlines, source code escrow or delivery, test reports, a list of third-party components (SBOM).","Run acceptance tests before going live, covering both functions and security items (account permissions, removal of default passwords, vulnerability scan, log check), signed off by the security contact.","Test data management: mask or pseudonymize production data with suitable tools. Keep records of test data requests and deletion, and define a maximum retention period (for example deletion within 30 days after the project ends).","Self-developed or significantly customized systems: use OWASP Top 10 or ASVS as development guidelines, with code reviews and SAST and DAST tools. For systems with very high protection needs, commission a third-party penetration test before going live and after major changes.","Outsourced development: agree in the contract on secure development, access control for the development environment, delivery documents and test reports, and review them regularly."],"audit":{"questions":["When you develop or buy a new IT service, how do you determine the security requirements?","How are legal or regulatory requirements (for example a data-protection law such as the GDPR) included in development?","Do you use a development standard, guideline or framework?","How are design changes controlled? How do you control outsourced development?","How is the development environment managed (commissioning, configuration, access, decommissioning)?","Which security items are included in the acceptance test before going live? Please show me the records.","Do you use production data for testing? How is it handled?"],"evidence":["Procedure for security requirements in system development and procurement","Security requirements checklist or requirement specification","Acceptance test records and security sign-off","Records of test data requests, masking and deletion","Security clauses in outsourced development contracts","Penetration test or code analysis reports"],"findings":["A new cloud system was introduced without any security requirements assessment. The user department simply subscribed with a credit card.","The acceptance test checked only functions and did not confirm that the default administrator account had been changed.","The test environment contains the complete production customer data, and access is not restricted.","A customized system developed by an external vendor has never had a security test.","Test data was not deleted after the project ended."]},"tip":"The cheapest security is the security you write into the RFP. If you ask before signing, the vendor says yes. If you ask after go-live, it becomes an extra budget item."},{"id":"5.3.2","title":"Requirements for network services","q_en":"To what extent are requirements for network services defined?","q_zh":"To what extent are requirements for network services defined?","objective":"Network services have different requirements for information security, quality of data transfer or management. It is important to know the criteria and the scope of use of the different network services.","req":{"must":["Requirements regarding the information security of network services are determined and fulfilled."],"should":["A procedure for securing and using network services is defined and implemented.","The requirements are agreed in the form of SLAs.","Adequate redundancy solutions are implemented."],"high":["Procedures for monitoring the quality of network traffic (e.g., traffic flow analyses, availability measurements) are defined and carried out. (A)"],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.8.21 | NIST CSF 2.0 PR.IR-04 | BSI IT-Grundschutz NET.1.1, NET.1.2 | NIST SP800-53r5 SA-3, SA-4, SA-17","change2027":"","explain":["Network services are connection and network-related services provided internally or externally, for example ISP internet lines, MPLS or SD-WAN lines, the ENX network, VPN services, DNS, cloud firewalls or managed Wi-Fi. The must requirement asks you to determine and fulfill their security requirements. The should requirements ask for a procedure for use and protection, agreement in SLAs, and adequate redundancy.","The difference from 5.2.7: 5.2.7 focuses on how you manage and segment your own network. 5.3.2 focuses on what the network services you use must achieve, especially when you buy them externally and need to put the requirements into the contract. Examples are the availability and repair time of the ISP, DDoS protection, the duty to report incidents, and the encryption method and admin rights of an SD-WAN provider.","High protection needs (availability) require monitoring the quality of network traffic, for example analyzing traffic with NetFlow or sFlow, and measuring availability and latency with PRTG, Zabbix or LibreNMS, so that you detect anomalies and capacity problems early. For a parts plant that delivers just in time, an EDI line outage can directly affect OEM call-offs, so the availability requirements should match the RTO in 5.2.8."],"practice":["Create a **network services inventory**: service name, provider, purpose, bandwidth, security features (encryption, DDoS protection), SLA content, contact person, redundancy method.","Sign SLAs with ISPs and line providers: availability, response and repair times for faults, notice of planned maintenance, reporting of security incidents, provision of reports.","Main sites use two ISPs, or a main line plus a 4G or 5G backup. Configure automatic failover on the firewall and test it regularly.","Procedure for using network services: which services may carry which classification levels, request and review of admin accounts, and configuration changes through the 5.2.1 process.","Deploy network monitoring (PRTG, Zabbix, LibreNMS, a cloud management platform) and set up alerts. Produce monthly availability and traffic reports and compare them with the SLA.","Review once a year whether the SLAs still meet business and RTO needs, and record provider breaches and improvements."],"audit":{"questions":["Which network services do you use? Which providers supply them?","What are the security requirements for these network services? Where are they written down?","Please show me the SLA with your ISP or line provider.","What is your redundancy if the main line fails? When did you last test the failover?","How do you monitor network quality and availability? Are there reports?","Do the SLAs meet your business continuity requirements?"],"evidence":["Network services inventory","SLA or service level clauses in the contract","Backup line configuration and failover test records","Network monitoring screens and availability reports","Annual SLA review records"],"findings":["The main internet line has no SLA. When it fails, the company can only wait for the normal customer service schedule.","The company claims to have a backup line, but it was never tested, and the actual failover failed.","Network availability is not monitored. An EDI outage was only noticed hours later when the customer called.","The network services inventory is incomplete. It is missing the line to a branch office and a cloud VPN service."]},"tip":"A backup line is like a fire extinguisher: if you buy one, you must test it. I recommend unplugging the main line once a quarter after working hours, and checking whether EDI and VPN switch over by themselves."},{"id":"5.3.3","title":"Return and secure removal of information assets from external IT services","q_en":"To what extent is the return and secure removal of information assets from external IT services regulated?","q_zh":"To what extent is the return and secure removal of information assets from external IT services regulated?","objective":"In order to ensure control over the information assets as the information owner, it is necessary that the information assets can be securely removed or are returned, if required, when terminating the IT service.","req":{"must":["A procedure for the return and secure removal of information assets from each external IT service is defined and implemented."],"should":["A description of the termination process is given, adapted to any changes, and contractually regulated."],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.5.23 | ISO 27017 CLD.8.1.5 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 10.2.6 | NIST CSF 2.0 ID.AM-08, GV.RR-04, PR.PS-03 | BSI IT-Grundschutz ORP.5, CON.2, CON.6, CON.9, ORP.2 | NIST SP800-53r5 MP-6, MP-8, SR-12","change2027":"","explain":["This is the **exit clause** control. Whether it is a cloud SaaS, IaaS, a hosted data center, outsourced IT or a cloud backup, when the service ends you must be able to get your data back and confirm that the data on the provider's side has been securely deleted. The key word in the must requirement is **each** external IT service. Every one needs a procedure, not only the big systems.","In practice, consider the following: whether the export format is usable (for example CSV, a database backup or a standard format, not just a view), the time and cost of the export, the retention period after the service ends, the deletion method and proof (for example a deletion certificate or a destruction certificate), whether copies in backups and disaster recovery sites are also deleted, and revocation of accounts and API keys.","The should requirement asks you to write the termination process into the contract and update it when things change. Many companies use cloud services with standard terms and cannot negotiate individual contracts. In this case, at least read and keep the provider's data retention and deletion policy, assess whether it is acceptable, and plan the export and verification steps in your internal procedure. This control is related to 5.3.1 (consider the exit already when buying), supplier management under 6.1.x, and the return of assets under 1.3.x."],"practice":["Add these fields to your **external IT services inventory**: data types and classification, export method and format, retention period after termination, deletion method, proof of deletion, location of the clause in the contract, person responsible for the exit.","Write an **External IT Service Termination Procedure** with a checklist: notice and timeline, complete data export and verification, revocation of accounts and keys, shutdown of integration interfaces, request for deletion with proof, update of the asset inventory.","Add exit clauses to contracts or RFPs: format and deadline for data return, duty to support migration, deletion deadline and proof, handling of backup copies, costs.","For cloud services with standard terms, keep the version of the provider's data retention and deletion policy and record your assessment conclusion.","Keep complete records of real terminations, for example export verification and deletion proof when you changed your cloud MES provider or retired an old file sharing service."],"audit":{"questions":["Which external IT services do you use? Does each one have an exit procedure?","If you ended this cloud service tomorrow, how would you get your data back? In which format?","How does the provider prove that the data has been deleted? What about the data in backups?","Does the contract have clauses on exit and data return?","Have you recently terminated an external service? Please show me the records."],"evidence":["External IT services inventory (including exit fields)","External IT service termination procedure and checklist","Data return and deletion clauses in contracts","Provider data retention and deletion policies","Export, verification and deletion proof from a real termination"],"findings":["Several SaaS services have no exit procedure at all.","After an old cloud backup service was terminated, deletion was not requested and there is no proof of deletion.","The contract does not define the data return format. Data can only be exported as PDF reports and cannot be migrated to the new system.","After a service was retired, the API keys used for integration were still valid."]},"tip":"When you start a relationship, you should already think about how a breakup would work, and the same is true for cloud services. Reading the exit clause carefully on the day you sign is much more effective than arguing with the provider on the day the service ends."},{"id":"5.3.4","title":"Protection of information in shared external IT services","q_en":"To what extent is information protected in shared external IT services?","q_zh":"To what extent is information protected in shared external IT services?","objective":"A clear segregation between individual tenants must be maintained to ensure that their information is fully protected within external IT services, preventing unauthorized access by other tenants.","req":{"must":["Effective segregation (e.g., segregation of customers) prevents access to own information by unauthorized users of other organizations."],"should":["The provider's segregation concept is documented and adapted to any changes. The following aspects are considered: – Separation of data, functions, customer-specific software, operating system, storage system and network; – Risk assessment for the operation of external software within the shared environment."],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27017 CLD.9.5.1, CLD.9.5.2 | NIST CSF 2.0 DE.CM-06, PR.IR-01, GV.SC-07 | BSI IT-Grundschutz OPS.2.1, OPS.2.2, OPS.3.1 | NIST SP800-53r5 SA-9, SC-4, SC-46","change2027":"","explain":["This control is about **multi-tenant** external IT services, for example public cloud SaaS, shared hosting, or shared platforms run by an MSP. The risk is that if segregation fails, other customers may see your data, or an attack on one tenant may affect other tenants. The must requirement asks for **effective segregation** that prevents unauthorized users of other organizations from accessing your information.","As a service user, most companies cannot verify cloud segregation themselves. In practice, you confirm it through evidence from the provider: for example ISO 27001 with ISO 27017 certificates, SOC 2 Type II reports, CSA STAR, TISAX labels, and the description of tenant segregation in the provider's security white paper. The should requirement asks that the provider's segregation concept is documented and considers separation of data, functions, customer-specific software, operating system, storage and network, as well as the risk of running external software in the shared environment.","The user side also has responsibilities (the shared responsibility model). For example, external sharing settings in M365, VPC and security group settings in Azure or AWS, and wrong tenant settings in a SaaS can all break segregation. If you are a service provider yourself (for example an engineering service company that hosts data for several OEMs), this control also applies to how you separate the data of different customers. This matches the separation of own networks and customer networks in 5.2.7."],"practice":["In your cloud service assessment process, ask providers for evidence of segregation: ISO 27001 and 27017 certificates, SOC 2 Type II reports, CSA STAR, security white papers. Record your assessment conclusion.","Check user-side settings: restrict external sharing in M365 or Google Workspace, use separate subscriptions or accounts per project in Azure or AWS, open VPCs and security groups only as much as needed, and check settings regularly with cloud security posture management tools (CSPM, Microsoft Secure Score).","If your company provides services to several customers (for example contract design or data hosting), write a **customer data segregation design description**: a separate folder, project or tenant for each customer, separate permission groups, separate encryption keys and network isolation.","Assess and approve external software that runs in the shared environment (for example vendor plug-ins or third-party apps), and limit its permissions.","Obtain and review the provider's segregation concept and audit reports again every year or after major changes at the provider."],"audit":{"questions":["Which shared external IT services do you use?","How do you confirm that the provider effectively separates the data of different customers?","Have you obtained and reviewed the provider's ISO 27017, SOC 2 or similar reports?","How do you check the tenant settings on the user side?","If you also provide services to several customers, how is data separated between customers?","How are third-party plug-ins or apps assessed before they run in the shared environment?"],"evidence":["Cloud service assessment records with provider certificates and reports","Provider segregation concept or security white paper","Tenant settings check records (CSPM, Secure Score)","Customer data segregation design description (if you are a service provider)","Approval records for third-party software"],"findings":["Several SaaS services are used, but no evidence of the providers' segregation was obtained or reviewed.","The company provides services to several OEMs, but all customer data is in the same folder with a shared permission group.","Cloud storage was configured for public access, which broke segregation.","The permissions of a third-party plug-in were not assessed, and the plug-in can read the data of the whole tenant."]},"tip":"A certificate is the starting point, not the finish line. However good the provider's segregation is, if you set a sharing link to \"anyone can view\", the segregation is gone."}],"summary":["5.1.1 All cryptographic procedures must meet recognized industry standards, and the complete key lifecycle must be managed. For high protection needs, key sovereignty must be ensured.","5.1.2 First inventory the transfer channels and set rules by classification. High protection needs require at least transport encryption, very high protection needs require content encryption. Remote access needs encryption, strong authentication and access management.","5.2.1 Changes to the organization, processes and IT systems must all consider security, with approval, impact assessment, testing and fallback. For high protection needs, verify during and after the change.","5.2.2 Use a risk assessment to decide whether to separate development, test and production. No development tools on production systems, and different accounts for different environments.","5.2.3 Malware protection covers endpoints, gateways, tamper protection and alternative measures for systems that cannot run antivirus. ISA 2027 deleted the should requirement on awareness training.","5.2.4 Assess which systems need logging, protect logs against alteration, check them regularly and escalate. High protection needs log remote access, very high protection needs log all access.","5.2.5 Gather vulnerability information, evaluate it with CVSS or similar methods, match it with assets and treat the risk. Patches must be tested, installed and verified, and systems that cannot be patched need risk-reducing measures.","5.2.6 Technical checks must be coordinated in advance, cover both internal and external views, be reported to management and be followed up. High protection needs require penetration tests, very high protection needs require regular vulnerability scans.","5.2.7 Segment the network based on risk, especially separating IT and OT and detecting lateral movement. High protection needs require device authentication, restricted management interfaces and handling of wireless and remote access risks.","5.2.8 Identify critical IT services and the business impact. The continuity plan must cover at least DDoS, ransomware, system failure and natural disasters. For high protection needs, each of the seven separate requirements needs its own evidence.","5.2.9 Backup and recovery concepts must consider dependencies and sequence. High protection needs include RPO, RTO, resources and spatial redundancy, plus restore tests. Very high protection needs require offline, immutable or isolated IAM backups and geographical redundancy.","5.3.1 Include security requirements when developing, buying or changing IT services, and verify them in acceptance. Avoid testing with production data. For very high protection needs, security test custom software.","5.3.2 Define security requirements for network services, agree them in SLAs and provide redundancy. High protection needs require monitoring of network quality.","5.3.3 Every external IT service needs a procedure for data return and secure removal, written into the contract.","5.3.4 Shared external IT services must effectively segregate tenants. Obtain the provider's segregation concept and evidence, and configure the user side correctly."]}]});
